"""MIT 18.05 final exam: only questions with an existing explanation."""
ITEMS=[]
def add(n,q,a,sol):ITEMS.append((n,q,a,sol))
add(1,r"Roll a fair six-sided die eight independent times. (a) Find the probability of no sixes. (b) Find the probability of exactly one six. (c) Find the expected number of sixes.",r"(a) $(5/6)^8$. (b) $8(1/6)(5/6)^7$. (c) $4/3$.",r"A non-six has probability $5/6$, so multiply eight such probabilities for (a). For (b), choose the one six's position in eight ways and multiply the independent event probabilities. Linearity of expectation gives $8(1/6)=4/3$ for (c).")
add(2,r"Let $X$ have density $kx^2$ for $1\le x\le2$, zero otherwise. (a) Find $k$. (b) Find its cdf. (c) Find $P(X<3/2)$. (d) Find its mean.",r"(a) $3/7$. (b) $F(x)=0$ below 1, $(x^3-1)/7$ on $[1,2]$, 1 above 2. (c) $19/56$. (d) $45/28$.",r"Normalization uses $\int_1^2x^2dx=7/3$. Integrating the normalized density up to $x$ gives the cdf. Evaluate it at $3/2$ to get $[(27/8)-1]/7=19/56$. Finally $E[X]=(3/7)\int_1^2x^3dx=45/28$.")
add(3,r"$X,Y$ are measured in dollars with $E[X]=5$, $\operatorname{Var}(X)=6^2$, $E[Y]=10$, $\operatorname{Var}(Y)=7^2$. Let $W=X+Y$. (a) Find its mean and units. (b) Find its variance assuming independence. (c) Instead assume covariance 21 and find its variance. (d) Under the latter assumption find correlation and its units. (e) For $Z=(X-5)/6$, find the mean and variance.",r"(a) 15 dollars. (b) 85 square dollars. (c) 127 square dollars. (d) $1/2$, dimensionless. (e) Mean 0, variance 1.",r"Means add without independence. The variance identity is $\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)=36+49+2\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)$, giving 85 or 127. Correlation is $21/(6\cdot7)=1/2$; its variance-derived units cancel. Centering and scaling give $E[Z]=(5-5)/6=0$ and variance $36/36=1$.")
# Problem 4 asks a subjective willingness-to-pay question; defer as a benchmark item.
add(5,r"A Pareto density is $f(x\mid\theta)=\theta x^{-\theta-1}$ for $x\ge1$, $\theta>0$. Given $n$ independent observations, find the MLE of $\theta$.",r"$\hat\theta=n/\sum_i\ln x_i$ when the denominator is positive; if every $x_i=1$, no finite MLE exists.",r"The log likelihood is $n\ln\theta-(\theta+1)\sum_i\ln x_i$. Its derivative vanishes at $n/\sum\ln x_i$ and its second derivative is $-n/\theta^2<0$. If all observations equal one, the likelihood grows as $\theta^n$ and has no finite maximum; this boundary case was omitted in the source.")
add(6,r"A condition affects 1% of a population. A test has false-positive rate 4% and false-negative rate zero. (a) Find prior odds of having the condition. (b) Given a positive result, find posterior odds.",r"(a) $1/99$. (b) $25/99$.",r"Prior odds are $0.01/0.99=1/99$. A positive result has likelihood ratio $1/0.04=25$, so posterior odds are $25/99$, about 1 to 4. This corresponds to posterior probability $25/124$.")
add(7,r"A robot starts uniformly at doors A,B,C,D in a cycle. A,B are orange and C,D blue. Each independent color scan is correct with probability 0.7 and incorrect with probability 0.3. (a) What is the prior probability it is at B? (b) After scanning orange, what is its posterior probability of B? (c) It moves one door right, wrapping D to A; what is the new probability it is at C? (d) What is the predictive probability of a blue second scan after the move?",
r"(a) $1/4$. (b) $7/20$. (c) $7/20$. (d) $1/2$.",r"The first-scan orange likelihoods are $(0.7,0.7,0.3,0.3)$. Multiplying uniform priors and normalizing gives posterior $(7,7,3,3)/20$. Moving rotates this to $(3,7,7,3)/20$, giving probability $7/20$ at C. The next blue likelihoods are $(0.3,0.3,0.7,0.7)$, whose dot product with the new prior is $(9+21+49+21)/200=1/2$.")
add(8,r"A stated candy color distribution is blue 20%, brown 20%, green 20%, orange 15%, red 15%, yellow 10%. In a sample of 1000, observed counts are respectively $180,190,185,160,165,120$. Test the stated distribution at significance 0.05. State hypotheses, the full numerical expression for the statistic, and the conclusion.",r"$\chi^2=235/24\approx9.7917$, 5 degrees of freedom, $p\approx0.0814$; do not reject the stated distribution.",r"Under $H_0$ the stated color probabilities hold; $H_A$ allows another distribution. Expected counts are $(200,200,200,150,150,100)$. The statistic is $20^2/200+10^2/200+15^2/200+10^2/150+15^2/150+20^2/100=235/24$. Six categories with known probabilities give 5 degrees of freedom. The upper-tail probability exceeds 0.05, so there is insufficient evidence to reject.")
add(9,r"Samples from 11 MIT and 11 Harvard students have mean/standard deviation $115,8$ and $110,6$, respectively. (a) What test can compare the means, with what assumptions and hypotheses? (b) Run a two-sided test at level 0.05; for hand arithmetic you may use $\sqrt{100/11}\approx3$. (c) Give a 95% confidence interval expression for the mean IQ of MIT students.",r"(a) Pooled two-sample t-test under independent normal samples with equal population variances; $H_0:\mu_M=\mu_H$, $H_A:\mu_M\ne\mu_H$. (b) $t=\sqrt{11}/2\approx1.6583$, df 20, $p\approx0.1129$; do not reject. (c) $115\pm t_{10,0.975}\,8/\sqrt{11}\approx115\pm2.228\,8/\sqrt{11}$.",r"The pooled sample variance is $(10\cdot64+10\cdot36)/20=50$. The squared standard error of the difference is $50(1/11+1/11)=100/11$. Dividing mean difference 5 by its square root gives $\sqrt{11}/2$. The source's hand approximation gives about 1.67 and the same decision. For the one-sample interval use the MIT sample standard deviation, 10 degrees of freedom, and the t critical value about 2.23. A small observed difference between sample variances alone does not prove the equal-population-variance assumption.")
add(10,r"In a departmental election, 50% support Sarah, 20% So Hee, and the remaining 30% is split evenly among four other candidates. (a) In a random poll of 100 people, estimate the probability at least 45% support Sarah. (b) A poll reports $53\%\pm5\%$ support at 95% confidence. Estimate its sample size using the usual worst-case polling rule.",r"(a) Approximately $\Phi(1)=0.8413$ without continuity correction. (b) $n\approx400$ by the rule $1/\sqrt n$; the conservative normal formula with 1.96 gives 385.",r"The sample fraction has mean 0.5 and standard deviation $1/(2\sqrt{100})=0.05$. Standardizing 0.45 gives -1, hence the normal approximation $P(Z\ge-1)$. The worst-case margin rule $0.05=1/\sqrt n$ gives 400. Using $1.96/(2\sqrt n)$ gives $n\ge384.16$, hence 385 after rounding up. Using the estimated proportion 0.53 instead of the worst-case 0.5 changes this convention slightly.")
add(11,r"Draw 100 independent observations from $N(\mu,1)$. Test $H_0:\mu=0$ against $H_A:\mu\ne0$ at significance 0.05. What is the power at $\mu=0.4$?",r"$1-[\Phi(-2.04)-\Phi(-5.96)]\approx0.9793$, using critical value 1.96.",r"The null acceptance region for the sample mean is $[-0.196,0.196]$, since its standard deviation is 0.1. Under mean 0.4, those boundaries standardize to -5.96 and -2.04. Power is one minus the probability in that interval, about $1-0.0207=0.9793$. The source mislabeled the acceptance interval as a rejection region in intermediate text.")
add(12,r"Data $(x,y)=(4,1),(-2,1.5),(0,0.5)$ follow $y_i=b|x_i-2|+e_i$ with independent errors. (a) What error assumptions justify least-squares fitting? (b) Find the least-squares estimate of $b$. (c) Plot the data and fitted curve.",r"(a) Zero mean errors, with equal variances for the usual homoscedastic least-squares model; normality is optional for estimation. (b) $\hat b=3/8$. (c) $y=(3/8)|x-2|$.",r"Zero mean errors center the model correctly; independent equal-variance errors give the usual ordinary least-squares justification. Identically distributed normal errors additionally justify likelihood-based inference but are not required to minimize squared errors. Let $a_i=|x_i-2|=(2,4,2)$. Differentiating $\sum(y_i-ba_i)^2$ gives $b=\sum a_iy_i/\sum a_i^2=(2+6+1)/(4+16+4)=9/24=3/8$. The source figure shows the V-shaped fitted curve with the data.")
add(13,r"""A sample of 36 times between trades has mean 7.0 and standard deviation 0.84. With no distributional assumption, 500 bootstrap sample means are generated and sorted. The smallest 50 are:
6.466,6.506,6.509,6.515,6.578,6.597,6.618,6.635,6.653,6.664,
6.670,6.672,6.685,6.696,6.703,6.707,6.713,6.721,6.727,6.727,
6.729,6.731,6.738,6.738,6.740,6.743,6.744,6.745,6.751,6.752,
6.759,6.760,6.768,6.774,6.775,6.777,6.778,6.780,6.784,6.784,
6.787,6.789,6.789,6.790,6.791,6.791,6.792,6.796,6.798,6.800.
The largest 50, ranks 451 through 500, are:
7.170,7.172,7.172,7.175,7.178,7.179,7.180,7.181,7.182,7.182,
7.182,7.186,7.195,7.202,7.202,7.205,7.206,7.210,7.216,7.219,
7.220,7.220,7.221,7.222,7.224,7.225,7.232,7.232,7.236,7.236,
7.243,7.244,7.245,7.251,7.253,7.258,7.261,7.263,7.266,7.273,
7.274,7.288,7.288,7.291,7.307,7.312,7.314,7.316,7.348,7.488.
Find a 90% percentile bootstrap confidence interval for the population mean, using the nearest-rank empirical quantiles as in the source solution.""",r"$[6.740,7.224]$.",r"The percentile interval uses empirical 0.05 and 0.95 quantiles. With 500 bootstrap means, the source's nearest-rank convention selects ranks 25 and 475. Reading those entries from the supplied lists gives 6.740 and 7.224.")
add('concept16',r"A two-sample t-test for equal population means rejects at significance 0.05. What are the odds that the populations have the same mean? Choose: (a) 19/1, (b) 1/19, (c) 20/1, (d) 1/20, (e) unknown.",r"(e) Unknown from the test result alone.",r"Frequentist testing gives probabilities of data under an assumed hypothesis. A significance level and rejection do not assign probabilities or odds to the hypotheses, so these odds cannot be inferred.")
